{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "What Is the Program Structure of C#?"}, {"type": "position", "content": 3}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 3}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use when talking about C# programming."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Understand important information about client software for the identity metasystem in spoken or written documents. "}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on principles, concepts, and features of general purpose computer programming languages."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob03re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Had Better, Ought to, and Might Want to for What Is Advisable"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<span class=\"strong\">Had better</span> is a modal verb used to say that if you don't do something, you will have a negative result.  <span class=\"strong\">Ought to</span> is used to give advice. <span class=\"strong\">Might want to</span> is used to recommend something."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Very good answer; you might want to keep it in mind all the time."}, {"type": "example", "content": "You had better name each namespace with a word or phrase that defines the process accurately."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<strong>Had better, ought to</strong>, and <strong>might</strong> are modal verbs.</br></br> <strong>Had better</strong> is used to say that if the person doesn't do a certain thing, they will experience a negative consequence. </br></br>  The structure you use with <strong>had better</strong> is the following:</br></br>  Subject + <strong>had better</strong> + verb in infinitive + complement.</br></br>  It is more natural to use <strong>had better</strong> with contraction.</br></br>   Example: <i>She'<strong>d better</strong> use lowercase in C#. </i>  </br></br><strong>Ought to</strong> is similar to should. The difference between them is that <strong>ought to</strong> can't be used in questions. </br></br>  The structure you use with <strong>ought to</strong> is the following:</br></br>  Subject + <strong>ought to</strong> + verb in infinitive + complement.</br></br>  Example: <i>You ought to use a short but accurate name for this namespace.</i></br></br>   <strong>Might want to</strong> is a polite way of saying that something is a good option. It is also followed by the base form of the verb.</br></br>    The structure you use with <strong>might want to</strong> is the following:</br></br>  Subject + <strong>might want to</strong> + verb in infinitive + complement.</br></br>  Example: <i>You <strong>might want to</strong>  save each project in a different folder.</i>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "In the comment section, you ought to add some information that tells you some specific information you need when modifying the code."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Things Not to Forget When Using C#"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "There are some essential characteristics that will help you write better in C#"}, {"type": "text", "content": "These are four important things to remember when writing C# code:</br></br>  \u2022 C# is case sensitive. Remember that you must use upper or lower case depending on the objects and variables; if you don't do it right, the program won't run.</br> \u2022 Finish any statements and expression with a semicolon (;), otherwise, you will have a syntactic error.</br> \u2022 Main method determines when the program execution starts.</br> \u2022 Different from Java, in C#, the program file name can be different from the class name.</br>"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "extensive "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "wide, big, or broad "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: These parts can be more or less extensive, depending on the complexity of the application or program we are developing."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob03aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "namespace declaration "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a section of the code in which programmers set a name to determine the scope of determined functions "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The parts that compose a C# program are namespace declaration, class, class methods, class attributes, main method, statements and expressions, and comments."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob03aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "enclose"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to contain or to determine between certain boundaries"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The namespace encloses one or several classes; it defines the scope of the program, and can also contain more namespaces."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob03aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "accurately "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "correctly, exactly or precisely"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adverb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You had better name each namespace with a word or phrase that defines the process accurately."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob03aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"con