Mr. Anderson</strong>: Welcome to this new lesson on programming. Today we will see the basics of C# program structure. Let's introduce our topic by remembering something Steve Jobs said. He said once that everyone, at least in this country, ought to learn how to program a computer; he said it teaches people how to think. What do you think about programming, Sophie?<br/>"}, {"time": "24000", "image": "enpe7n5le03ob03re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<strong>Sophie</strong>: I believe that programming is greatly important because you can make something completely new from scratch.\n<br/><strong>Mr. Anderson</strong>: Very good answer; you might want to keep it in mind all the time. Well, let's begin with our matter. The parts that compose a C# program are namespace declaration, class, class methods, class attributes, main method, statements and expressions, and comments. These parts can be more or less extensive, depending on the complexity of the application or program we are developing."}, {"time": "58000", "image": "enpe7n5le03ob03re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "The namespace encloses one or several classes; it defines the scope of the program, and can also contain more namespaces. You had better name each namespace with a word or phrase that defines the process accurately. The classes contain the definitions of data and methods and their relationships among them. In the comment section, you ought to add some information that tells you some specific information you need when modifying the code; this line doesn't affect the program structure or functioning. </br></br>"}], "coverimg": "enpe7n5le03ob03re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n5le03ob03re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 4, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can list the parts that compose a C# program."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "c